    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d50">

                <pb facs="0149" n="77" sameAs="frus:frus1882/pg77"/>

                <head>No. 50.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Trescot to Mr. Frelinghuysen</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Special Mission of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Santiago</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1882-02-24"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >February</hi> 24, 1882</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Frelinghuysen</hi>, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Washington:</hi></p>
                <p>Chili will not modify peace terms, alleging they are, in deference to the United
                    States, better than the offer at Arica and Lima. Terms are <pb facs="0150"
                        n="78" xml:id="pg78"/> extreme, but Chili is so strong, Peru so weak, that
                    mere friendly intervention will not have effect. Telegraph at once definite
                    instructions what to say and what to do. No use remaining here. Shall I go to
                    Peru and to Bolivia? Shall Blaine remain or return?</p>
                <closer><signed>TRESCOT.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
